Finding: {"title":"Standby-queue cancel button has no error handling — silent failure on delete","severity":"LOW","area":"Error Handling / UX","files":["src/components/features/mvp-v2/pages/student-standby.tsx"],"detail":"`handleCancel` (lines 144-150) does `await fetch(...DELETE...)` with no `res.ok` check and no try/catch. If the DELETE request fails (network error, 404, 500), the fetch promise rejects unhandled (React logs an unhandled rejection) or resolves with a non-2xx status that is never inspected, and `fetchEntries()` is still called (refetching unchanged data) with zero user-facing feedback. This matches the project's own documented anti-pattern in CLAUDE.md ('Silent Catch Blocks Prohibited' — user sees no error indication).","impact":"A student clicking 'Cancel' on a standby request that fails server-side sees the entry silently remain in their list (or briefly disappear from optimistic UI expectations then return) with no error toast/message explaining why, leading to confusion or duplicate cancel attempts.","recommendation":"Wrap in try/catch, check `res.ok`, and surface a toast/error message on failure (mirroring the pattern already used correctly in `handleAdd` in the same file, and in the parallel `StudentStandbyPanel.handleLeave` implementation).","confidence":"medium"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

